Compositing manager notes.
==========================

The compositor is built and enabled by default (--disable-compositor turns it
off at build time, "xfwm4 --compositor=off" or the /general/use_compositing
xfconf setting at run time).

Windows are drawn with OpenGL when the driver allows it and with XRender
otherwise. /general/use_gl_compositing switches between the two, and
XFWM4_GL_BACKEND=glx makes the OpenGL renderer use GLX instead of EGL.

README.md describes the OpenGL renderer, its settings, and when it falls
back to XRender.

vblank and tearing
------------------

Sync to vblank is selected with the "--vblank" command line option or the
/general/vblank_mode xfconf setting, read at startup only:

  $ xfwm4 --replace --vblank=auto &
  $ xfconf-query -c xfwm4 -p /general/vblank_mode -s auto

  auto      sync every frame to the screen (default)
  adaptive  stop syncing when the frame rate falls below the refresh rate;
            needs GLX (XFWM4_GL_BACKEND=glx) or XRender and
            GLX_EXT_swap_control_tear, otherwise same as auto
  off       no sync

  glx       XRender only: sync through GLX (libepoxy at build time)
  xpresent  XRender only: sync through the Present extension (libXpresent at
            build time)

With the OpenGL renderer, "glx" and "xpresent" behave like "auto".

"xfwm4 --version" lists which of these were enabled at build time.

Backdrop blur
-------------

The OpenGL renderer blurs the already-composited scene behind configured
translucent window categories. Panels such as xfce4-panel can show frosted
glass without capturing the root window themselves. MiniOS taskbar previews
are identified by a dedicated X11 property; override-redirect menus, tooltips
and notifications by their EWMH _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE. Window types are checked
again on PropertyNotify, since GTK may set them after window creation.

  /general/blur_enabled              boolean, enabled by default (master switch)
  /general/blur_docks                boolean, enabled by default
  /general/blur_transparent_windows  boolean, enabled by default (managed windows)
  /general/blur_previews             boolean, enabled by default (MiniOS tasklist)
  /general/blur_alt_tab              boolean, enabled by default (Alt+Tab popup)
  /general/blur_menus                boolean, disabled by default (popup menus)
  /general/blur_tooltips             boolean, disabled by default (tooltips)
  /general/blur_notifications        boolean, disabled by default (notifications)
  /general/blur_radius               1..64 pixels, default 14
  /general/blur_strength             0..100 percent, default 80

The blur is a Dual Kawase GPU pass over the selected window's region plus its
sampling margin. An opaque window hides its own blurred backdrop. If the
OpenGL shader/FBO path is unavailable, normal translucency is preserved.
Disabling blur for one category does not disable it for others; XRender is
unaffected.

The GL blur region excludes xfwm4 compositor-owned shadows. For GTK
client-side decorations, the compositor clips it to the surface excluding
_GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS (left, right, top, bottom), including late property changes.
A client may instead declare _XFWM4_BLUR_CONTENT_RECT as four CARDINAL values
[x, y, width, height] relative to its own X11 drawable. MiniOS tasklist
previews publish this automatically; their soft native Gaussian shadow is
drawn outside the content and is never included in the popup backdrop blur.
Tasklist and window-switcher shadows follow /general/show_popup_shadow and
the standard /general/shadow_opacity and shadow offset parameters. No separate
preview shadow control or CSS drop shadow is used. The preview/type markers
are checked again on PropertyNotify, independently of the blur setting.
Clients lacking both hints keep the standard whole-window blur area:
raw ARGB alpha alone does not identify which pixels are a drop shadow.

Window switcher and taskbar highlights
------------------------------

The window switcher (default shortcut Alt+Tab) can use the shared Peek path through
/general/cycle_peek_mode (0 off, 1 dim, 2 hide) and
/general/cycle_peek_opacity (0..100). The original window frame works
independently through /general/cycle_draw_frame. The MiniOS tasklist may
request the exact same window-switcher frame using the root-window property
_XFWM4_HOVER_FRAME containing two CARDINAL values:
  [hovered client XID (zero hides), fill enabled (zero or one)]
A one-value request is also accepted and enables the fill.
The renderer drops the frame on lost taskbar heartbeats after 850 ms, and
continues working with XRender or compositing entirely disabled. Both
renderers ignore taskbar Peek requests while an Alt+Tab cycle owns Peek.
Wireframes have an empty X11 input shape in both their Cairo and Xlib paths:
an outline or its transparent interior must never intercept a thumbnail's
activation/close click when the selected window overlaps the preview popup.
The hover hint is handled even when the WM starts with --compositor=off.

Pointer-routing regression (requires Xvfb, python3-xlib, DBus and xfconf):
  sh tests/check-hover-input /absolute/path/to/xfwm4 xrender
  sh tests/check-hover-input /absolute/path/to/xfwm4 off
Both use private X11/DBus/xfconf sessions and exercise popup clicks through
the interior and outer edge with the frame fill enabled and disabled.

The tasklist and switcher have one themed #xfwm-tabwin presentation. Their
background color/opacity, corner radius, border, card spacing and content
padding are rendered by shared xfwm-tabwin-style.h code. Window Manager Tweaks
exposes cycle_background_custom/color/opacity, cycle_corner_radius,
cycle_border_width, cycle_spacing and cycle_content_padding; the tasklist
retains its corresponding preview-* values. Appearance does not require GL.

Retained minimize snapshots
---------------------------

OpenGL and XRender minimize/restore animations use saved images, including
client pixels, even after X11 has unmapped the window. The image is reused on
restore until the window has finished its normal X11 repaint. Cached snapshots
are bounded to 64 MiB per screen; older entries are evicted as needed. GL stores
textures; XRender stores ARGB pixmaps in the X server, without CPU readback.
Scale + Fade works on both backends. Magic Lamp remains GL-only.

Opening and closing animations
------------------------------

/general/window_effect selects None (0), Scale + Fade (1, default), or Fade
(2). /general/window_duration is 50–1000 ms, default 160 ms. These effects
work on both compositor backends; no compositing retains immediate opening and
closing. Opening waits for the first usable image.
Closing removes the X11 client immediately and paints a detached image with
XID-only stacking anchors, without retaining Client or CWindow pointers.
Closing images share the 64 MiB cache budget with minimize snapshots and are
released at completion or cancellation, including workspace/backend changes.

tests/check-window-lifecycle <xfwm4-path> opengl|xrender <output-dir> exercises
the effects in an isolated Xvfb/DBus session. Software GL requires a separate
test-only build; production fallback policy is unchanged. For real GPU
diagnostics, XFWM4_ANIMATION_TEST_SESSION=1 plus XFWM4_ANIMATION_CAPTURE_DIR
enables cropped framebuffer captures. Remove both variables after the test.

Backdrop blur and window opacity
--------------------------------

The final backdrop mix uses blur_strength multiplied by the window's native
_NET_WM_WINDOW_OPACITY factor. At zero opacity no blur pass is painted. This
keeps fading tasklist popups and other translucent UI from leaving a strong
rectangular blur behind their disappearing contents. If the mix shader is
unavailable, the ordinary premultiplied-alpha texture path uses the same weight.

Tasklist Peek
----------------

The tasklist sends _XFWM4_GL_PEEK updates to the compositor when the
pointer enters an individual thumbnail. Both renderers transition other
windows' rendered opacity without altering the X11 client state; the selected
window is painted on top from the live pixmap or, if iconified, its cached
minimization snapshot.  The taskbar and popup are redrawn above the selection.
The mode is off by default, and automatically expires after 1.1 seconds
without a heartbeat. `_XFWM4_PEEK_SUPPORTED` advertises support to tasklist
versions that know about XRender Peek; the historical request name is retained.

The property is an array of four CARDINAL values on the X11 root window:
  [client XID, mode, opacity percent for the other windows, duration in ms]
Mode 0 disables Peek, mode 1 dims the other windows, and mode 2 hides them in
the composed rendering output. Switching between thumbnails crossfades the raised
windows; the normal display is restored on exit.  Heartbeat messages do not
repaint the entire desktop when Peek is stationary.  If minimization effects
are disabled, both backends save a non-animated snapshot at iconification so a
previously minimized window can be previewed.  As with minimize animations,
retained snapshots share a 64 MiB per-screen memory budget.  Missing/evicted
snapshots cannot be displayed until the client maps again.

The budget is reserved before allocating active effect images, not only after
minimization finishes. Close-image maintenance admits only images that fit;
it never evicts another image merely to fill the background cache. Existing
same-size images are refreshed in place, and deferred client damage and native
opacity changes remain pending independently of live texture rebinding.

Blur scenes are composed bottom-to-top so a backdrop never samples an opaque
foreground window. Partial repaint areas include the transitive closure of
all affected blur sample rectangles before any layers are drawn. The switcher
advertises its entire painted surface, including internal layout padding;
native compositor shadows remain outside that drawable.

Blur expansion is part of current visible frame damage, both for buffer-age
history and EGL swap rectangles. Historical backbuffer repairs are accumulated
separately and are never recorded as new current damage. Detached closing
images retain foreground-covered pixels in bottom-up blur scenes, since a
blurred surface can sample them before the foreground is composed.

The switcher also checks its final GTK requisition against the monitor. A
bounded scrolling viewport handles client counts, fonts and large padding
values that cannot fit even with minimum-size icons; saved settings are not
rewritten. Keyboard selection continues through the native GTK focus path.

Focused regressions (private Xvfb/DBus/xfconf sessions):
  sh tests/check-compositor-regressions /absolute/xfwm4 snapshot output-dir
  sh tests/check-compositor-regressions /absolute/xfwm4 blur output-dir
  sh tests/check-compositor-regressions /absolute/xfwm4 switcher output-dir
  sh tests/check-compositor-regressions /absolute/xfwm4 closing-blur output-dir
  XFWM4_DAMAGE_DEBUG=1 sh tests/check-compositor-regressions /absolute/xfwm4 damage output-dir
  sh tests/check-compositor-damage
The first two require accelerated GL or a separate software-enabled test build.
They read actual GL framebuffer pixels using XFWM4_ANIMATION_CAPTURE_ALL=1,
which is honored only with the existing test-session and capture-directory
variables. It captures naturally requested paints, without adding a repaint
timer. The switcher geometry test also works with the production XRender path.

Integration checks: tests/check-peek <xfwm4-path> opengl|xrender <output-dir>
The tests use an isolated Xvfb display and private DBus/xfconf, never the
current user desktop.  Software GL may be blacklisted in the normal MiniOS
binary: run the OpenGL case with an accelerated test X server or in a separate
test-only build that permits software GL. The XRender case exercises Scale +
Fade and verifies that Magic Lamp remains inactive on that backend.
